{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "pitchzazz: real-time audio engineering walkthrough",
  "description": "Twelve stops demonstrating real-time-systems understanding, DSP mechanism and correctness, and a controlled two-language performance comparison. C++ leads throughout since the presentation is on C++ skills; the Rust prototype appears only as a short history detour after stop 1. Each step is short enough to say out loud during a live demo; the last line of each points at the doc section with the full depth for anyone reading async. Steps use both a line-number guess and a text `pattern` CodeTour can use to relocate the step if the surrounding file has changed since this tour was written. Eight steps include a diagram (`.tours/assets/*.svg`) for the mechanisms that are easier to see than to narrate. A companion glossary for every acronym used below lives at the top of `docs/ALGORITHMS.md`.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "1. Never block, allocate, or lock on the audio thread",
      "file": "cpp-plugin/Source/PluginProcessor.cpp",
      "line": 171,
      "pattern": "Everything below is real-time-safe by construction",
      "description": "This is `processBlock`, the actual audio callback in the C++/JUCE plugin: downmix into a fixed-size stack buffer (no heap allocation), then push into a lock-free FIFO (First In, First Out queue) — `juce::AbstractFifo`, atomic indices, no mutex, audited before use the same way the original Rust prototype's `ringbuf` was. Push/pop copy fewer samples than requested rather than blocking when the FIFO is full or empty. No lock, no blocking send, no allocation — the three things that would turn this callback into a source of audible glitches, avoided by construction.\n\n![Producer/consumer pipeline: audio callbacks only push/pop a lock-free ring buffer; the worker thread does all the real work](data:image/svg+xml;base64,)\n\nFull story: this class's doc comment in `PluginProcessor.h`, and `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`'s \"Real-time audio safety\" section, which this C++ side mirrors."
    },
    {
      "title": "2. Detour: the original Rust prototype",
      "file": "crates/pitch-cli/src/main.rs",
      "line": 242,
      "pattern": "Input callback: copy into the ring buffer",
      "description": "Quick history detour: pitchzazz started as a Rust CLI, `pitch-cli`, with cpal audio callbacks pushing into a lock-free ring buffer (`ringbuf` — its actual source was read, not its docs, to confirm `.split()` really gives atomic-index Producer/Consumer halves, not a mutex in disguise; full story in `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`'s Dependency Audit section). This is that original callback, the direct ancestor of the C++ version in the previous stop — same real-time-safety discipline, one language earlier."
    },
    {
      "title": "3. How pitch detection works: NSDF peak-picking",
      "file": "cpp-plugin/Source/DSP/PitchDetector.cpp",
      "line": 181,
      "pattern": "DetectedPitch PitchDetector::detect",
      "description": "This is the McLeod Pitch Method (MPM), a modified autocorrelation technique. A quick energy gate skips silence, then the block is autocorrelated via FFT (Fast Fourier Transform; Wiener–Khinchin: |X(f)|² → IFFT, an Inverse FFT) and normalized by an energy term into the NSDF (Normalized Square Difference Function) curve — bounded and comparable across lags, which plain autocorrelation isn't. The first peak past the trivial τ=0 self-correlation peak that clears a fixed clarity threshold is refined to sub-sample precision by quadratic interpolation, then converted to Hz.\n\n![Autocorrelate via FFT, normalize into the NSDF curve, then pick the first peak past the trivial zero-lag peak that clears a clarity threshold and refine it by quadratic interpolation](data:image/svg+xml;base64,)\n\nFull story: this file's own comment on the FFT-normalization convention (why the JUCE/rustfft mismatch is safe here — `docs/FINDINGS.md` #10), and `docs/COMPARISON.md`'s FFT-convention section."
    },
    {
      "title": "4. How scale quantization works: nearest-in-scale search",
      "file": "cpp-plugin/Source/DSP/Scale.cpp",
      "line": 55,
      "pattern": "int nearestInScaleMidi",
      "description": "Pitch class = note mod 12 (wrapped positive). If that pitch class is already in the scale, the note passes through unchanged. If not, the search checks outward by semitone distance 1 through 6 — below first, then above, at each distance — and returns the first pitch class that belongs, by reading the scale's own interval table directly rather than a reconstructed lookup.\n\n![A MIDI note's pitch class is checked against the scale; if it doesn't belong, search outward by semitone distance, checking below then above, until one does](data:image/svg+xml;base64,)\n\nThis design avoids a real bug that stalled the original Rust prototype: pitch detection and shifting both worked, but the original hand-built a note lookup table sized to assumptions that didn't hold for every scale, silently corrupting some scales' \"nearest note\" results. The fix — reading the scale's own interval table directly instead of reconstructing one — is what's shown here. Full story: `crates/pitch-core/src/scale.rs`'s module doc, `docs/FINDINGS.md` #4."
    },
    {
      "title": "5. Decoupling DSP block size from the host's callback size",
      "file": "cpp-plugin/Source/DSP/CorrectorWorker.cpp",
      "line": 166,
      "pattern": "if (filled < blockSize)",
      "description": "The pitch detector needs a fixed block size; the host's `processBlock` doesn't guarantee one. `CorrectorWorker` accumulates whatever it pulls from the input FIFO into `analysisBuffer` and only runs one correction pass once exactly `blockSize` samples are ready — a short `wait(1)` poll otherwise — decoupling the two.\n\nDirect port of the identical pattern in the original Rust CLI (`pitch-cli/src/main.rs`'s `if filled < BLOCK_SIZE`). Full story: this file's class doc."
    },
    {
      "title": "6. Perf investigation: budget miss to measured fix",
      "file": "docs/PERFORMANCE_LOG.md",
      "line": 33,
      "pattern": "Measured `PitchCorrector::process()` time",
      "description": "First live run: 40-60% over the real-time budget. Per-stage timing (not a single top-line number) showed it was entirely the pitch-shift stage — a leftover oversampling factor of 32 that had never been independently measured. Dropping it to 8 fixed it with margin.\n\nFull story: the four chained entries starting here, ending in a measured (not estimated) end-to-end latency number, plus `docs/FINDINGS.md` #5."
    },
    {
      "title": "7. How the phase vocoder shifts pitch",
      "file": "cpp-plugin/Source/DSP/PitchShifter.cpp",
      "line": 69,
      "pattern": "void PitchShifter::shiftPitch",
      "description": "Per-bin phase difference from the previous frame gives each bin's true instantaneous frequency (unwrapped mod 2π via the `qpd` trick). That frequency and its magnitude are relocated to bin' = round(k·shift) — literally moving spectral content to shift pitch — while phase is accumulated coherently across frames (`phaseSum`) so consecutive frames stay phase-locked. An inverse FFT and windowed overlap-add (hop = frame size / oversampling) resynthesize the signal.\n\n![Each frame's phase difference gives true frequency, which is relocated to a shifted bin and accumulated across frames before an inverse transform and windowed overlap-add](data:image/svg+xml;base64,)\n\nFull story: this file's comments on the FFT-normalization convention and the C++/Rust frame-size divergence; `docs/COMPARISON.md`'s FFT size constraints section."
    },
    {
      "title": "8. How TD-PSOLA shifts pitch",
      "file": "cpp-plugin/Source/DSP/PSOLAPitchShifter.cpp",
      "line": 69,
      "pattern": "void PSOLAPitchShifter::placeGrainAt",
      "description": "TD-PSOLA stands for Time-Domain Pitch-Synchronous Overlap-Add. The actual pitch-shift mechanism: analysis marks sit on a grid spaced one pitch period apart; synthesis marks sit on a *different* grid, spaced period/shiftRatio apart. Each synthesis mark reads a windowed grain from the analysis mark at or before it (floor-aligned) and writes that same content back at its own position. Shifting up packs synthesis marks closer together, so several reuse the same analysis grain; shifting down spaces them farther apart, so some analysis grains go unused entirely. Reusing or skipping content at a different spacing than it was recorded at is what changes pitch — nothing about a grain's own content is ever re-pitched.\n\n![Analysis marks sit on a fixed-period grid; synthesis marks sit on a different grid; each synthesis mark reads the analysis grain at or before it and writes it at its own position, reusing grains when shifting up](data:image/svg+xml;base64,)\n\nKnown, accepted limitation: snapping to a single bucket causes an audible crackle/beat artifact on non-stationary audio — a cross-fade fix was tried, shipped, and reverted (it didn't help by ear and may have hurt) — full diagnosis in `docs/FINDINGS.md` #19-20 (the floor-alignment rounding bug that predates it is #18)."
    },
    {
      "title": "9. How Varispeed + WSOLA shifts pitch",
      "file": "cpp-plugin/Source/DSP/VarispeedShifter.cpp",
      "line": 13,
      "pattern": "void VarispeedShifter::shiftPitch",
      "description": "A third, deliberately time-domain algorithm. WSOLA (Waveform Similarity Overlap-Add) time-stretches the signal by the shift ratio first — pitch-preserving, duration-changing — then a variable-rate resample (cubic Hermite interpolation) restores the original duration. That resample step is the actual pitch shift, the same physical effect as varispeed-ing a tape reel: unlike the other two engines, formants shift right along with pitch here, on purpose.\n\n![WSOLA time-stretches the signal by the shift ratio first, pitch-preserving but duration-changing; then a variable-rate resample restores the original duration and is the actual pitch-shift mechanism, shifting formants along with pitch](data:image/svg+xml;base64,)\n\nTwo real bugs found and fixed by ear on live vocal audio, not by an automated metric: a correlation search that degenerated to matching near-zero lag (fixed with a hard minimum-improvement threshold, not just a distance penalty — the first attempt actually made the artifact worse), and an O(sampleRate²) search cost invisible at 44.1kHz but a real dropout at 96kHz. Full story: `docs/ALGORITHMS.md` section 7, `docs/FINDINGS.md` #22-24."
    },
    {
      "title": "10. Three algorithms, three hot-swappable engines",
      "file": "cpp-plugin/Source/DSP/PitchEngineRegistry.cpp",
      "line": 10,
      "pattern": "const std::vector<EngineDescriptor>& availableEngines()",
      "description": "All three engines are registered right here, hot-swappable at runtime — and now three genuinely distinct algorithms: `rust-ffi` and `native-cpp` both implement the phase vocoder (one ported line-for-line from the other, see `docs/COMPARISON.md`), `psola-cpp` reasons about time-domain grains, and `varispeed-wsola` reasons about resampling rate. Three different ways to solve the same problem, not three implementations of one idea.\n\n![Phase vocoder shifts pitch by moving FFT bins, with latency fixed to the analysis window; TD-PSOLA shifts pitch by re-spacing time-domain grains at pitch marks, with latency bounded but pitch-dependent](data:image/svg+xml;base64,)\n\nThe measured payoff: TD-PSOLA's latency is ~19-46% lower than the phase vocoder depending on rate and tuning, because it scales with pitch period, not a fixed sample count — though re-tuning either engine has flipped that ordering before, so treat it as a snapshot, not a fixed fact (`docs/ALGORITHMS.md` section 5). The cost: a real, disclosed reconstruction artifact on non-stationary audio on both PSOLA (`docs/FINDINGS.md` #19-20) and Varispeed (`docs/FINDINGS.md` #23-24) — the price of any algorithm that depends on a period or correlation estimate rather than just a shift ratio."
    },
    {
      "title": "11. Hot-swap architecture + the 20-30x illusion",
      "file": "cpp-plugin/Source/DSP/CorrectorWorker.cpp",
      "line": 74,
      "pattern": "void CorrectorWorker::requestEngineSwap",
      "description": "A new pitch-correction engine (Rust, C++ phase vocoder, C++ TD-PSOLA, or C++ Varispeed) is handed off via one atomic pointer exchange, picked up between blocks, never mid-`process()`.\n\n![Hot-swap: a new engine is stored into one atomic pointer, picked up by the worker thread between blocks, then blended in via a crossfade over four blocks](data:image/svg+xml;base64,)\n\nThe first swap discarded the outgoing engine's warmed-up internal state, causing an audible glitch no unit test or plugin validator caught — `docs/FINDINGS.md` #14. Separately: a first-pass Rust-vs-C++ benchmark looked 20-30x apart. It was a debug-vs-release build mismatch, not a language difference — full numbers in `docs/COMPARISON.md`."
    },
    {
      "title": "12. One validator isn't enough",
      "file": "docs/FINDINGS.md",
      "line": 28,
      "pattern": "juce::Thread` destructor asserts (UB) if destroyed while still running",
      "description": "This row: a real crash bug — UB is Undefined Behavior — that passed PluginVal at strictness 5 cleanly, then was caught by Apple's own `auval` — run specifically because relying on one validator felt insufficient. Direct evidence two independently-implemented tools catch genuinely different bug classes.\n\nFull ledger: every other row in this table, plus the tooling breakdown further down."
    }
  ]
}
